     llooccaallee — get locale-specific information

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     llooccaallee [--aa | --mm]
     llooccaallee --kk lliisstt [_p_r_e_f_i_x]
     llooccaallee [--cckk] [_k_e_y_w_o_r_d _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The llooccaallee utility is supposed to provide most locale specific informa‐
     tion to the standard output.

     When llooccaallee is invoked without arguments, it will print out a summary of
     the current locale environment, subject to the environment settings and
     internal status.

     When llooccaallee is invoked with the _k_e_y_w_o_r_d arguments, and no options are
     specified, it will print out the values of all keywords specified, using
     the current locale settings.

     The following options are available:

     --aa      Print names of all available locales.  While looking for locales,
             llooccaallee will respect the PATH_LOCALE environment variable, and use
             it instead of the system's default locale directory.

     --cc      Print the category name for all selected keywords.  If no key‐
             words are selected, print the category name for all defined key‐
             words.

     --kk      Print the names and values of all selected keywords.  If no key‐
             words are selected, print the names and values of all defined
             keywords.

     --mm      Print names of all available charmaps.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     The special (FreeBSD specific) keyword lliisstt can be used to retrieve the
     human readable list of all available keywords.  If so, a prefix string
     can be defined to limit the amount of keywords returned.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The llooccaallee utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     setlocale(3)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The llooccaallee utility conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1-2004 (“POSIX.1”).  The
     LC_CTYPE, LC_MESSAGES and NLSPATH environment variables are not inter‐
     preted.

BBUUGGSS
     Since FreeBSD does not support _c_h_a_r_m_a_ps in their POSIX meaning, llooccaallee
     emulates the --mm option using the CODESETs listing of all available lo‐
     cales.

